Implement CGDisplayIsAsleep, which Qt calls while updating its screen list.

THE APPLICATION HAD BEEN EXITING SILENTLY AND I HAD NO NAME FOR IT. Two thirds of runs produced
29 lines, four Qt internal windows, and a clean exit=0: no ObjC exception, so no "Terminating
app" line, and nothing in the log to grep for.

Qt keeps its own diagnostics behind a switch, and app-drive.sh applies TRACE_ENV AFTER the
variables it unsets, so QT_LOGGING_RULES survives the QT_* unset added in c371bbd2. With it on,
the answer was one line:

    dyld: lazy symbol binding failed: Symbol not found: _CGDisplayIsAsleep
      Referenced from: /Applications/CMake.app/Contents/PlugIns/platforms/libqcocoa.dylib

immediately after "qt.qpa.screen: Updating screens with 1 online displays".

LAZY IS WHY IT LOOKED LIKE NOTHING. A missing symbol bound eagerly fails at load and says so; one
bound lazily binds when it is CALLED, so the process starts, does real work, and aborts at the
call with no exception and an exit status of 0. This symbol was first in the 13 recorded in #227,
where I wrote it was NOT proven to block anything because the application ran past them. It ran
past them because it had not called them yet.

FALSE IS THE TRUE ANSWER, not a placeholder: nothing here puts a display to sleep, so a caller is
correctly told no, and a display that does not exist is not asleep either.

MEASURED AFTER: no lazy bind failure remains, and the application gets further, 29 lines to 73
with logging on. What is left is the intermittent colour crash, 2 of 8, which is a different bug
and still open, plus an early clean exit that is next.

--- a/CoreGraphics/CGDirectDisplay.m
+++ b/CoreGraphics/CGDirectDisplay.m
@@ -270,6 +270,20 @@
     return display >= 1 && display <= [[current screens] count];
 }
 
+/*
+ * FALSE IS THE TRUE ANSWER, not a placeholder: nothing in this port ever puts a display to sleep,
+ * so a caller asking whether one is asleep is correctly told no. A display that does not exist is
+ * not asleep either, and answering TRUE for it would read as a powered down screen rather than an
+ * absent one.
+ *
+ * Qt calls this while updating its screen list and it is bound LAZILY, so a missing symbol was not
+ * a load failure: the process ran, drew nothing, and aborted the moment the call was reached, with
+ * no ObjC exception and an exit status of 0. See task #227.
+ */
+boolean_t CGDisplayIsAsleep(CGDirectDisplayID display) {
+    return FALSE;
+}
